# The graphs this model composes, as the lane sets of one pool. One row per graph: what its nodes
# are, what its edges are, and what a loop in it means.
#
# A `VALUES` literal, because it is a contract
#   Worked out from the queries, it could never report that a graph has nothing behind it, and the
#   claimant row is that case: it is declared here and supplies no edges at all, which is the
#   finding rather than an omission.
#
# Three lane sets, not three lanes
#   A `laneSet` is a division claimed complete, so three lanes would put every composition in
#   exactly one, and most compositions touch more than one graph: a relation reading a part and its
#   factor is in the layer graph and the unit graph, and that is what a factor is. BPMN allows
#   several lane sets over one process for this reason.
#
# Only one can be the containment
#   A tree gives each node one parent, so only one of these can be the nesting a diagram draws; the
#   others need showing some other way. That is why `examples/diagramming` takes the graph as a slot
#   rather than quietly picking one, and why a caller that names none does not run.

# `cycle_sense` says which meaning of the word a row uses
#   This relation asks one question of three graphs, what a cycle in it means, and the word has
#   four meanings in these queries, set out in `composition/descent.sqlc`'s header: a loop that
#   ignores direction, layers that reach each other following direction, the window a supply is
#   live in, and the loop you can walk round. A column that does not say which cannot be compared
#   down its own rows, and comparing them is all a three-row contract read by one emitter is for.
#
# The layer row answers the meaning that ignores direction
#   Both meanings hold of the layer graph, and they are not interchangeable.
#   `checks/layers_move_together` rules on the one that follows direction, layers that reach each
#   other. `checks/jagged_layer` holds the loops that ignore direction at zero, which
#   `algebra/cycle_space` states as no loop exactly where no fusion's parts overlap, and
#   `examples/graphs` asserts on every run. A diamond separates them: following direction it has
#   no loop, so no two layers reach each other and `layers_move_together` finds nothing, while
#   ignoring direction it has one loop, and `jagged_layer` accuses. This column answers the meaning
#   that ignores direction because that is the one the drawing itself carries:
#   `examples/diagramming` counts the emitted call graph's loops and holds them against the model's.
#
# `governed_by` is checked against the rule that holds each graph
#   `examples/graphs` holds each graph to the rule it uses itself, so a graph cannot cite just any
#   relation and have the emitter carry the citation into a reader's document.
-- the three graphs this model composes, as data an emitter chooses between.
SELECT * FROM (VALUES
  ('layers',    'a layer, keyed (filing, layer)', 'a part: a layer composed from a layer',
                'undirected',
                'a leaf reached down two branches of one fusion, which counts it twice',
                                                                              'checks/jagged_layer'),
  ('units',     'a unit',                          'a conversion: a factor from unit to unit',
                'undirected',
                'expected, and required to close: converting round it returns what it started with',
                                                                              'checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close'),
  ('claimants', 'a claimant: software, an agent, a role, a person',
                'delegation, and nothing files it',
                'directed',
                'unknown, and not a fault: mutual signing authority is a legitimate cycle',
                                                                              NULL)
) AS g(graph, node_is, edge_is, cycle_sense, a_cycle_is, governed_by)
